<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miguel Miramón</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miguel Miramón was a Mexican conservative general who headed the conservative government during the Reform War from 1859 to 1860, one of the youngest men to hold the presidency. Defeated by the liberals, he went into exile and later returned to serve Emperor Maximilian. Captured with Maximilian at Querétaro, he was executed with him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-mejia">Tomás Mejía</a> on 19 June 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miguel-miramon.jpg" alt="Miguel Miramón" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miguel_Miramon_uniform.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Miguel Gregorio de la Luz Atenógenes Miramón y Tarelo was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 29 September 1831, into a traditional family of partial French heritage. He entered the military college in February 1846. During the American invasion he fought at the Battle of Molino del Rey and the Battle of Chapultepec, where he was wounded and taken prisoner.</p><p>After the liberal Plan of Ayutla triumphed in 1855, Miramón took part in conservative revolts. In October 1856 he was second in command of a revolt at Puebla and, with a thousand soldiers, defended the city for 43 days against an army of 6,000. He joined the forces of Félix María Zuloaga when the Plan of Tacubaya began the Reform War in January 1858.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Miramón's victories in 1858, among them the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> on 10 March, earned him the press name Young Maccabee. After a moderate conservative faction overthrew Zuloaga in December 1858, a junta elected Miramón president, and Zuloaga formally passed the presidency to him on 31 January 1859. He led two sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> had his liberal government. The first was abandoned by 29 March 1859, and the second failed when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> captured his naval squadron at the Battle of Antón Lizardo on 6 March 1860.</p><p>At the Battle of Calpulalpan on 22 December 1860, his 8,000 troops met 16,000 liberals under Jesús González Ortega, and Miramón lost. The liberals entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 1 January 1861. Miramón fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and was received at the Spanish court on 2 December 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He returned during the Tripartite Expedition and was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, then re-entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in July 1863 and offered his services to the regency of the Second Mexican Empire. Emperor Maximilian sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> to study the Prussian army. Miramón came back on 9 November 1866 and took command of the western military district. He was routed at San Jacinto on 1 February 1867 and took refuge at Querétaro, where he became the leading general during the siege.</p><p>Colonel Miguel López opened the gates of Querétaro to the Republicans on the night of 14 May, in exchange for gold, and Miramón was shot in the face. Maximilian, Miramón and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-mejia">Tomás Mejía</a> were tried for violating an 1862 decree against traitors and invaders and sentenced to death. On 19 June 1867, at the Cerro de las Campanas, Maximilian yielded Miramón the place of honour in the centre. Before the shooting Miramón read a brief statement disavowing the charge of traitor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miguel Miramón” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Napoleon III recalled his troops in 1866–1867, and republican armies besieged Maximilian at Querétaro, where he surrendered in May 1867 and was shot on 19 June together with the conservative generals <b>Miguel Miramón</b> and Tomás Mejía.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Maximilian stayed, was besieged at Querétaro and captured, and was shot with generals <b>Miramón</b> and Mejía on 19 June 1867.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-mexican-empire">Second Mexican Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-queretaro">Santiago de Querétaro</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.35,-99.14&amp;year=1854" aria-label="Mexico City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1871 1447 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miguel Miramón"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2321 1717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.35,-99.14&amp;year=1854">What was here in 1854? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
